00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.289 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  five years ago I was sitting on the  couch in my mom's living room when a  Campbell's soup commercial caught my  attention Campbell's was releasing its  Star Wars branded soup and a commercial  began with a dad feeding his little son  which Star Wars shaped noodles in the  spoon same and I quote Cooper I am your  father the camera then zooms out so  Cooper's second parent is also seen who  then says emphatically no Cooper I am  your father my heart melts as I quickly  realize that without singing word  Campbell's was making a tremendous  statement by choosing to have two dads  in its commercial from that moment on I  knew that I company a company could sell  products while still taking a stand now  five years ago it had already been three  years since I first come out we're  coming out is always a continuous  process and I was slowly reconciling the  religious and traditional views of my  Caribbean household with my own sexual  orientation while we've made tremendous  strides in representing LGBT individuals  in the media today they're so few and  far between a common belief is that  brands should stay out of social issues  and many companies have chosen to stay  silent on issues going on today this  opinion is changing however and consumer  expectations are shifting instead in a  world where US consumers state that they  hold more trust in businesses than in  the government consumers are looking to  businesses to use their influence and  their power to make a difference in the  world and neutral is no longer neutral  it's choosing to stay silent in fact  according to Kohn communications 87% of  consumers say they were purchase a  product because the company supported an  issue that they cared about and 76% said  they were refused to purchase a product  if a company held use contrary to their  beliefs these statistics are evident in  brands that have gained support because  of their social values such as Evelyn  and brands that have lost support such  as chick-fil-a prior to my time at  Stanford GSB I spent my career in  marketing research and strategy digging  into the mindsets of diverse consumers  from all different age groups  demographics and backgrounds I've  interviewed hundreds of individuals and  one of the biggest learnings that I've  had is that individuals especially  younger consumers are expecting  businesses to be more personal and more  real to showcase this I'll share with  you an ad from the brand always show me  what it looks like to run like a girl  show me what it looks like to fight like  a dog  now throw like a girl so do you think  you just insulted your sister no I mean  yeah insulted girls but not my sister my  name is Dakota and I'm 10 years old show  me what it looks like to run like a girl  throw like a girl  fight like a girl  what does it mean to you when I say run  like a girl  you mean when fast as you can  this video has been viewed over 90  million times making it one of the most  watched ads of all time this is  incredible for an ad that doesn't even  showcase the product and following  launch up the ad always his Twitter  followers tripled in three months and in  a study conducted seventy percent of  women and sixty percent of men said that  the ad changed their perception of the  phrase like a girl in the end it wasn't  about the actual product it was about  what the always brand stood for since  then many other brands have followed  suit by choosing to join social  movements Starbucks for example promised  to hire ten thousand refugees following  president Trump's immigration ban in  2017 and following an incident in 2018  where two black men were arrested for  using a Starbucks bathroom the company  closed eight thousand retail locations  in order to hold racial bias training  for all of its employees and lastly  following Nikes very controversial colin  kaepernick ad Fast Company reported that  Nike gained a hundred and sixty three  million in earned media and gotten a 31  percent boost in sales the following  week in the end it wasn't about the  coffee or the shoes it was about what  these brands stood for and actions like  these will become even more relevant  especially as consumer demands in  corporate social responsibility coincide  with the fact that according to the US  Census Peru 50% of the US population  will be non-white by 2045 now I've often  gotten the question how do we know if a  brand is being authentic versus just  trying to make money I've pondered this  for a while with the conclusion that I  actually don't hear  whether a brand is doing this to sell  more products or to promote is social  values if they do it well then the end  result is still the same Nike will  promote racial justice in our country  always will further further the  conversation about gender equality and  Campbells will normalize LGBT  relationships and if they make money  even better when I sit back and I  reflect on the Campbell Soup commercial  today I think about the power of  representation it's nearly impossible to  fathom what you can become without  seeing others who have walked in your  shoes I personally remember the  Campbell's soup commercial so I can only  imagine that countless other queer  people that the commercial impacted  perhaps individuals who grew up in much  more conservative environments than I  was in or the power of the commercial on  straight individuals helping to further  normalize the fact that gay families  exists and are happy and are just like  there's moments like watching the  Campbell Soup commercial broadens  perspectives it brings a sense of  belonging and it can even change the  world  so as future business leaders I'm asking  that use your influence and your power  to promote change the actions you take  as overt as having an ad promoting  social issues where as subtle as just  having diverse people in your ads do  make a difference and can benefit both  your business and society and lastly  we're all consumers so I'm asking that  you dig internally to find the values  that are most important to you for me  its diversity or for you it could be  anything from workers rights to public  health to the environment then I ask  that you buy from companies that do well  in supporting those values and that you  stop buying from companies that  disappoint you then expect more expect  that brands show up at protests that  they pay employees for your wages and  that the people who work there or  as diverse as the people they serve the  movement is growing but the movement  depends on you thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

